A(('p', '13.2 Neighboring parables. The Great Banquet, the Lowest Seat, the Ten Virgins, the Sheep and the Goats and the Wise and Foolish Builders are each read in their own study of the Parable Studies. This passage makes no use of their meanings, and they make none of this one.'))
A(('p', '13.3 Final Synthesis. Jesus is journeying toward Jerusalem, teaching, when one asks whether there are few that be saved. He gives no number. He says to strive to enter in at the strait gate, because many will seek and not be able. The master of the house rises up and shuts the door, those outside knock and are answered, “I know you not whence ye are,” and their claim of eating, drinking and teaching in their streets is answered with “depart from me, all ye workers of iniquity.” There is weeping and gnashing of teeth when they see Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and all the prophets in the kingdom of God and themselves thrust out, and people come from the four directions and sit down there. The last shall be first, and the first last. Jesus names none of the parts. The comparison says that seeking is spoken of as not enough, that a shut door is not opened by knocking, and that the order at the table is reversed.'))
